\f0\fs22 \cf2 What happened next was procurement.\
The recommendation said destroy, so destroy was costed, and it was costed properly, because that is what the department was for. Incineration of a facility at that depth is not a fire; it is a sustained thermal event requiring oxygen feed, containment, air handling and a certification regime, and it came in at four times the alternative. The alternative was concrete. Concrete was cheaper.\
And\u8212 ?this is the part that matters, and nobody in the chain noticed it, and it has cost the world rather more than the four times\u8217 ? difference ever would have\u8212 ?concrete produced a receipt.\
A destroyed thing has to be certified destroyed by somebody who went down and looked, and that somebody has a name, and that name goes on a form, and forms are kept. A sealed thing is certified by an invoice. Sixty-one thousand cubic metres, delivered, poured, signed for by a foreman on a docket, filed by a clerk who thought it was footings.\
The instruction went out as one line in a board paper, executed inside the week across a dozen installations by people who never met Hardy and never asked. Nobody was evacuated, because nobody was told there was anything to evacuate from, and the last shift went down at the usual hour.\
And that is how both of them survived. Intact, catalogued, dry, fifty metres down under ten metres of dome, sealed in the most conscientiously engineered dark on the continent: the cure, and the other thing, the one the platform had been built for in the first place. Preserved perfectly. Waiting a couple of hundred years for a man with a drill and a deadline.\
Jamison Hardy destroyed the cure for every human disease by keeping it in mint condition, and neither he nor anybody else ever noticed.\
He went up in the car a little after eleven.\
He was, by then, quite certain what he was. He had saved the entire world that day, and had done it alone, and had done it at a cost he would carry by himself, and no one would ever know. That was the curse of it. That was, he thought, looking out at the lights of a city that had no idea, the curse of the true hero: to be owed everything by everybody, forever, and to be thanked by nobody, ever.\
He was a little tearful about it, in the car, on the way up.\
He was right, of course. Nobody ever found out. Nobody ever will. Two centuries later a man will recite that report\u8217 ?s finding at a much better lunch, as his own opinion, having never in his life heard of a single person who was in that room; and another man will shrug and say, apparently not, and go on with his day. There is no file. There is no whisper. There is one docket for sixty-one thousand cubic metres of concrete, in a box, in a storage facility that has changed hands nine times.\
He was thanked by nobody, ever, and he was owed everything, forever, and he was correct on both counts, and we are the only ones who will ever be in a position to tell him so.}